---
import StarlightPage from '@astrojs/starlight/components/StarlightPage.astro';

// Figures and limits come from the repo README, bench/README.md and bench/coding/README.md; keep them in step.
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const blob = 'https://github.com/lemun/kitzur/blob/main/';
---

<StarlightPage
	frontmatter={{
		title: 'Benchmarks',
		description:
			'What Kitzur has been measured against so far: a scripted demo, simulated sessions, offline replay and a coding harness. Live-model validation is pending.',
	}}
>
	<aside class="kz-status">
		<p>
			<strong>Status: pending live validation.</strong> Nothing on this page comes from a live model
			solving a task. These checks show how Kitzur compacts. They do not show task success rates.
		</p>
	</aside>

	<h2 id="kinds">Four kinds of evidence</h2>
	<table>
		<thead>
			<tr><th>Evidence</th><th>Runs against</th><th>Checks</th><th>Does not show</th></tr>
		</thead>
		<tbody>
			<tr>
				<td>Demo recording</td>
				<td>A scripted agent and a stub upstream, not a model</td>
				<td>The passthrough, compact and reuse actions in response headers, and what <code>kitzur status</code> reports</td>
				<td>Anything about model output</td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
				<td>Simulated sessions</td>
				<td>Scripted clients and a mock server that counts rendered prompts and returns scripted completions</td>
				<td>Whether requests fit, prompt tokens processed, planted-fact markers, and tool call/result pairing</td>
				<td>Comprehension, inference speed, billing savings or task quality</td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
				<td>Replay (<code>kitzur replay</code>)</td>
				<td>Your captured request bodies, run through the engine offline</td>
				<td>Request sizes before and after, and whether a fresh engine produces the same result as a live one</td>
				<td>Responses: no upstream call is made</td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
				<td>Coding harness</td>
				<td>One minimal tool agent, run directly and through Kitzur against the same endpoint</td>
				<td>Hidden acceptance tests on the code it writes, with corrections and tool-output pressure</td>
				<td>Real-model results yet: it has only run in mock mode, which tests the harness itself</td>
			</tr>
		</tbody>
	</table>

	<h2 id="simulated-session">A 46-step simulated session</h2>
	<p>
		The <code>qa46-ref</code> scenario is a synthetic browser-automation session with a 100,000-token
		window and 32,000 tokens reserved for output, measured October 2, 2026 with the Qwen3.6-27B
		tokenizer. The direct client has no compaction; the OpenCode arm is an offline simulation of its
		compaction mechanics with placeholder summaries.
	</p>
	<table>
		<thead>
			<tr><th>System</th><th>Steps completed</th><th>Prompt tokens, all attempts</th><th>Planted facts retained</th></tr>
		</thead>
		<tbody>
			<tr><td>Direct client, no compaction</td><td>10/46</td><td>429,881 (partial session)</td><td>Not comparable</td></tr>
			<tr><td>OpenCode compaction mechanics, offline simulation</td><td>46/46</td><td>2,492,849</td><td>Not evaluated</td></tr>
			<tr><td>Kitzur, default 100k preset</td><td>46/46</td><td>1,710,787</td><td>7/7</td></tr>
		</tbody>
	</table>
	<p>
		The direct request at step 11 needs 74,445 prompt tokens. With the output reservation the server
		allows 68,000, so it is rejected. Through Kitzur that step is 28,251 tokens and accepted. Of the two
		completed sessions, Kitzur processed 31.4% fewer prompt tokens. Totals include rejected attempts
		and simulated summary requests. The direct total covers a partial session, so it is not a savings
		baseline.
	</p>
	<p>
		<a href={`${blob}bench/examples/readme-qa46.json`}>Recorded measurements</a> ·
		<a href={`${base}/docs/benchmarks/#readme-demonstration`}>Reproduce this run</a>
	</p>

	<h2 id="limits">Limits</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>Every session here is synthetic and scripted. None is a recording of a real user.</li>
		<li>Fact checks look for planted markers, current values and superseded values. A marker can be present without a model understanding it.</li>
		<li>The OpenCode comparison simulates its mechanics. No LLM writes those summaries, so their quality is not measured.</li>
		<li>Summaries are mechanical and limited by the budget. Under pressure, facts and user text can be shortened.</li>
		<li>
			Historical Gobstopper figures predate scenario sanitation. Do not mix them with the current
			figures; see <a href={`${base}/compare/`}>compare</a>.
		</li>
		<li>A better prefix hit ratio alone does not mean less work. At 32k, an earlier Kitzur run had worse prefix reuse than the OpenCode comparator.</li>
	</ul>

	<h2 id="pending">Pending live validation</h2>
	<p>
		The coding harness is ready for live runs against a Chat Completions endpoint with a real context
		limit, but none has run yet: they are waiting for model capacity. Those runs will compare paired
		task outcomes, direct and through Kitzur. They will report usage the server returns and mark it
		unknown when the server omits it. Until then, no claim on this site covers real-model task quality.
	</p>
	<p>
		<a href={`${base}/docs/benchmarks/`}>Benchmark methodology</a> ·
		<a href={`${blob}bench/coding/README.md`}>Coding task evaluation</a>
	</p>
</StarlightPage>

<style>
	.kz-status {
		border: 1px solid var(--sl-color-accent);
		border-radius: var(--kz-radius);
		padding: 0.75rem 1.25rem;
	}
</style>
